# Solid Process Patterns

Nested files under `app/models/<context>/` are **not** table models. They are processes, domain constant modules, or serializers. Flattened Active Record stays at `app/models/*.rb` (`User`, `UserSession`).

Do not reintroduce `app/models/core/`, `*::Record`, mappers, entities, adapters, or facades.

## Processes

```ruby
class User::Registration < Solid::Process
  deps do
    attribute :mailer, default: UserMailer
  end

  input do
    attribute :email, :string
    attribute :password, :string

    normalizes :email, with: ->(value) { value.strip.downcase }
    validates :email, :password, presence: true
  end

  def call(attributes)
    rollback_on_failure {
      Given(attributes)
        .and_then(:create_user)
        .and_then(:create_email_confirmation)
    }
      .and_then(:send_email_confirmation)
      .and_expose(:user_registered, %i[user])
  end
end
```

- Name after the use case. Keep table models flattened so they do not collide (`UserEmailConfirmation` vs `User::Email::Confirmation`).
- Declare `deps` only when injecting (`mailer`). Authenticated flows take `user` on `input` and validate `instance_of: User, is: :persisted?`.
- Talk to Active Record directly. `create` / `update` when validation can fail (merge `user.errors`, `Failure(:invalid_input, input:)`). `create!` / `update!` for child records that must not fail.
- On domain failure: `input.errors.add(...)` then `Failure(:specific_type, input:)`.
- Keep `deliver_later` **outside** `rollback_on_failure`.
- Add Input error keys under `activemodel.errors.models.<process/path>/input`.

Canonical: `User::Registration`. Also see `User::Authentication`, `User::Email::Change`, `User::Profile::Update`.

## Domain constants

```ruby
module User::Email
  FORMAT = URI::MailTo::EMAIL_REGEXP
  CONFIRMATION_TOKEN_EXPIRES_IN = 24.hours
end
```

Modules for formats, expirations, and token helpers (`User::Token.generate` / `.digest`). Not Active Record.

## Serializers

```ruby
class User::Serializer
  include JSONAPI::Serializer

  attributes :id, :first_name, :last_name, :email
end
```

Wrap the Active Record from the process result in the controller: `User::Serializer.new(user)`.
